.TH "XERPY" 1 "May 2026" "3.0" "xerpy manual"
.SH NAME
\fBxerpy\fR — generate example strings matching a regular expression

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBxerpy\fR [\fB-w\fR | \fB-e\fR] [\fB-s\fR] \fIREX\fP [\fIN\fP [\fISEED\fP]]

.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBxerpy\fR generates random example strings that match a given (simple,
Unix-style) regular expression. It is the inverse of \fBrexpy(1)\fR,
which infers regular expressions from example strings.

.PP
\fIREX\fP is the regular expression to generate strings for.

.PP
\fIN\fP is the number of strings to generate (default: 1).

.PP
\fISEED\fP is the random seed to use (default: derived from the current
time).

.SH SUPPORTED REGULAR EXPRESSION SYNTAX
\fBxerpy\fR understands a Unix-style subset of regular expression syntax:
.IP \(bu 2
Character classes, e.g. \fB[a-z0-9]\fR, including negation
(\fB[^0-9]\fR) and POSIX classes (\fB[:digit:]\fR, \fB[:alpha:]\fR,
\fB[:upper:]\fR, \fB[:lower:]\fR, \fB[:punct:]\fR, \fB[:xdigit:]\fR, \fB[:space:]\fR,
\fB[:blank:]\fR, \fB[:graph:]\fR, \fB[:cntrl:]\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
Alternation, e.g. \fB(cat|dog|bat)\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
Quantifiers \fB*\fR, \fB+\fR, \fB?\fR and \fB{m,n}\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\&.\fR, matching any (printable) character.
.IP \(bu 2
Escapes \fB\\d\fR (digits, same as \fB[0-9]\fR), \fB\\w\fR (word characters,
similar to \fB[a-zA-Z0-9_]\fR), \fB\\s\fR (whitespace), and \fB\\t\fR, \fB\\r\fR,
\fB\\n\fR, \fB\\f\fR, \fB\\v\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
Unicode-style property classes \fB\\p{...}\fR/\fB\\P{...}\fR (negated),
e.g. \fB\\p{alpha}\fR, \fB\\p{digit}\fR, \fB\\p{upper}\fR, \fB\\p{punct}\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB^\fR at the start and \fB$\fR at the end, as start/end anchors. These
are effectively always present whether written or not.
.IP \(bu 2
Alternations without parentheses are treated as if they had
parentheses, specifically \fBA|B\fR is treated as \fB^(A|B)$\fR --- not
\fB(^A|B$)\fR\&.

.SH OPTIONS
\fB-w\fR, \fB--weighted\fR
.br
  Choose among alternation branches (e.g. \fBa|b|ccc\fR) weighted by each
  branch's estimated cardinality, so that branches admitting more
  possible strings are chosen more often.

.PP
\fB-e\fR, \fB--even\fR
.br
  Choose among alternation branches uniformly, regardless of each
  branch's cardinality. This is the default; it explores rare
  branches (e.g. narrow literal alternatives) more than \fB-w\fR would.

.PP
\fB-s\fR, \fB--seed\fR
.br
  Print the random seed used, as \fBSeed: SEED\fR, before the generated
  strings.

.SH EXAMPLES
1) \fBxerpy '[A-Z]{2}[0-9]{3}'\fR

.PP
Generate one random string matching \fB[A-Z]{2}[0-9]{3}\fR\&.

.PP
2) \fBxerpy '[A-Z]{2}[0-9]{3}' 5\fR

.PP
Generate five such strings.

.PP
3) \fBxerpy '[A-Z]{2}[0-9]{3}' 5 42 -s\fR

.PP
Generate five such strings using seed \fB42\fR, printing the seed first.

.PP
4) \fBxerpy '([0-9]|[a-z]{3})' 10 -w\fR

.PP
Generate ten strings, favouring the \fB[a-z]{3}\fR branch over \fB[0-9]\fR
since it admits many more strings.

.SH SEE ALSO
\fBrexpy(1)\fR
